## STILL MISSING

### Cube motions and the even/odd vertex split (§5.2)

The cube-motions section needs a concrete cube: 8 vertices, its 12 edges as adjacency,
and 24 motions as a listing. The n-gon machinery of `std/permutation/dihedral.b4m`
(adjacency-preserving permutations, exhaustion by walking the vertices, listing =
powers ++ reflected powers) would apply, but "six faces on top × four rotations" is a
geometric count with no n to induct on — everything would be finite case work. The
even/odd vertex split is prose for the same reason. Still marked in place
(`aata/5.2-dihedral-groups.md:245`, "counting + a concrete S_4").

### Well-founded / lexicographic induction

Judson's parity LEMMA ("the identity is a product of an even number of
transpositions") is a terminating-rewrite argument on a lexicographic measure —
product length, then the position of a symbol's last occurrence — and 2b4m has
ℕ-induction only. Its four rewrite identities are statements about transpositions
named by the points they move; the first, (ab)(ab) = 1, is proved
(`std/permutation.b4m` `transpositionIsAnInvolution`), and the other three are
pointwise case analyses of the same kind, so what blocks the lemma is the
well-founded induction alone.

The lemma is therefore ASSUMED as `axiom identityIsEvenLength`
(`aata/5.1-permutation-groups.md:471`) exactly as Judson states it. It is the only
assumption of the section's own making: `--axioms` lists it beside the std postulates
the section rests on, and nothing else from that file. Everything downstream is proved.

