## Open, smaller

### A theorem SCHEMA is checked only at a concrete instantiation

A file whose only content is a schema reports "0 theorems proven" and exits 0 — the
body is checked at its instantiations, so a schema that no instance ever exercises is
as unverified as a hole while looking green. `std/permutation/listing.b4m` therefore
ends with a concrete instance (`splittingOffTheIdentity`) to drive the whole chain.
Not a bug by the current design, but worth a diagnostic. (Contradicts the older memory
note saying strict check verifies a schema at declaration.)

